ntdll: an IPv4 option macOS refuses on an IPv6 socket is set as its IPv6 counterpart.

Windows applies IPPROTO_IP options set on an AF_INET6 socket to the socket's IPv4 traffic, which on a
dual-stack socket (IPV6_V6ONLY off) is its IPv4-mapped peers. macOS refuses every IPPROTO_IP option on an
AF_INET6 socket with EINVAL, so setsockopt() answers WSAEINVAL (10022).

Forza Horizon 6 opens its UDP game socket that way before matchmaking: [::]:3074, IPV6_V6ONLY 0,
SIO_UDP_CONNRESET and SIO_UDP_NETRESET (0035), then IP_DONTFRAGMENT 1 and IP_TTL 255. Both options fail,
the game closes the socket, tries 5250 and 5002 the same way and never joins a session (WINEDEBUG=+winsock
on CrossOver Preview 41303, 2026-11-13). On Highball's arm64 route (arm64-kk-wine11.18-r4, M5 Pro,
macOS 27.3 beta, 2026-11-14) online play fails the same way: no UDP socket of the game stays open.

When the host refuses IP_DONTFRAGMENT, IP_TTL, IP_MULTICAST_TTL or IP_MULTICAST_LOOP on an AF_INET6 socket
with EINVAL, ntdll sets or reads its IPv6 counterpart (IPV6_DONTFRAG, IPV6_UNICAST_HOPS, IPV6_MULTICAST_HOPS,
IPV6_MULTICAST_LOOP) instead. On macOS the counterpart reaches IPv4-mapped traffic: a datagram sent to
::ffff:127.0.0.1 with IPV6_UNICAST_HOPS 200 reaches an IPv4 socket with TTL 200 (64 by default), natively and
under Rosetta. It also reaches the socket's IPv6 traffic, which on Windows, an IPv4 version would not. For
IP_TTL and multicast options only a header field changes, but IPV6_DONTFRAG changes what a send is permitted
to do: after IP_DONTFRAGMENT 1, a datagram larger than the path MTU sent to an IPv6 peer fails with
WSAEMSGSIZE (10040), whereas Windows leaves the socket's IPv6 traffic alone and fragments it. When sent to an
IPv4-mapped peer it fails the same way, which is what IP_DONTFRAGMENT asks for. Read back before anything set
it, the hop limit answers -1 ("the system default"); IP_TTL then reports the TTL in use (net.inet.ip.ttl, 64)
and IP_MULTICAST_TTL 1, as Windows reports its own defaults, instead of 4294967295. A host that accepts the
IPv4 option never takes this path, nor does an AF_INET socket or any error but EINVAL. IP_TOS is left alone:
no program here needs it, and Windows ignores it by default.

Measured over loopback (MTU 16384) with a dual-stack socket and SO_SNDBUF 65535 (macOS caps a UDP datagram at
9216 bytes otherwise): a 20000-byte datagram to ::1 or to ::ffff:127.0.0.1 is sent without IP_DONTFRAGMENT and
fails with 10040 with it, on x64-crossover26.3-r32 and arm64-kk-wine11.18-r4 with this patch; 12000 bytes is
sent either way. Natively, IPV6_DONTFRAG gives EMSGSIZE in the same cases, arm64 and under Rosetta. On a real
network the limit is the path MTU, at least 1280 bytes for IPv6. This assumes a program that sets
IP_DONTFRAGMENT sizes its datagrams to stay under that limit, so that only a datagram it would not send anyway
is refused; FH6's datagram sizes were not measured.

Checked with a test program that opens the socket as the game does, reads three of the options before anything
sets them, then sets and reads back all four, sends to ::ffff:127.0.0.1 and checks an AF_INET socket's own
options (26 checks; tests/dualstack-udp/udpopts.c). On x64-crossover26.3-r33 it fails 12
(these and SIO_UDP_NETRESET); with this patch and 0035, none. The same change is MacGameFix's wine-11.21 0026
(MathiasKowoll/MacGameFix#73, by the same author), where FH6 played online on CrossOver Preview 41303. Not
sent upstream.

